{"schemaVersion":1,"key":"fundamentals/tools","track":"engineers","lang":"en","dir":"ltr","title":"Tool Calling","objectives":{"lang":"en","items":["Explain how a model requests a tool call, and who actually executes it","Describe what a tool schema contains, and where it comes from","Explain why a tool's name and description decide whether it gets used","Follow one real tool call through the request and the response","Identify tasks that need a tool rather than more prompting"]},"prerequisites":[{"key":"fundamentals/llms","number":1,"title":"LLM Fundamentals","drawn":true,"track":"engineers","lang":"en"}],"previous":{"key":"fundamentals/rag","number":3,"title":"RAG & Embeddings","drawn":true,"track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"next":{"key":"fundamentals/memory","number":5,"title":"Memory","drawn":true,"track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"toc":[{"id":"what-is-a-tool","text":"What is a Tool?","depth":2},{"id":"tool-calls-grow-context","text":"Tool Calls Grow Context","depth":2},{"id":"why-tools-exist","text":"Why Tools Exist","depth":2},{"id":"tool-schema","text":"Tool Schema","depth":2},{"id":"tool-call-in-action","text":"Tool Call in Action","depth":2},{"id":"what-the-model-receives","text":"What the Model Receives","depth":3},{"id":"what-you-ask","text":"What You Ask","depth":3},{"id":"what-the-model-generates","text":"What the Model Generates","depth":3},{"id":"what-the-host-machine-does","text":"What the Host Machine Does","depth":3},{"id":"what-the-model-answers","text":"What the Model Answers","depth":3},{"id":"example-tools","text":"Example Tools","depth":2},{"id":"key-terms","text":"Key Terms","depth":2},{"id":"summary","text":"Summary","depth":2},{"id":"references","text":"References","depth":2},{"id":"-articles","text":"📄 Articles","depth":3}],"body":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"fundamentals/llms","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"LLM Fundamentals"}]},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"fundamentals/training","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Training LLMs"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"fundamentals/rag","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"RAG & Embeddings"}]},{"type":"text","text":" covered three things: what a model is, how it was trained, and how to put your own data in front of it. In all of that, the model is still only reading. In this module, the model starts "},{"type":"emphasis","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"doing"}]},{"type":"text","text":" things: it reads a file, runs a command, or calls an API."}],"lead":true},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"This is the mechanism underneath every agent. So it is worth the effort to understand it exactly."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"what-is-a-tool","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"What is a Tool?"}]},{"type":"image","number":"4.1","title":"An LLM reaching out through tools","note":"Tools are how a model reaches outside its own head. It searches the web, does maths or runs code, and then comes back with an answer.","src":"course-images/fundamentals/tools.webp","alt":"An LLM reaching out through tools","width":440,"height":242},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"A "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"tool"}]},{"type":"text","text":" is a function that you write. It is an ordinary Python function with a name, some inputs and a return value. There is nothing special about it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"This is the part that people get wrong: "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"the model never runs your function."}]},{"type":"text","text":" It cannot. It has no computer. The only thing it can do is write a message that says \"I would like to call "},{"type":"code","text":"read_file"},{"type":"text","text":" with "},{"type":"code","text":"filename=\"main.py\""},{"type":"text","text":"\". Something on your side reads that message and runs the function. Then it gives the return value back."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"tool-calls-grow-context","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Tool Calls Grow Context"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"fundamentals/llms","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"LLM Fundamentals"}]},{"type":"text","text":" introduced the context as a stack of messages. A tool call adds two new kinds of message to that stack. This picture shows the whole flow:"}]},{"type":"image","number":"4.2","title":"The context of a single tool call","note":"One tool call, in order. The system prompt declares the tool before anyone speaks. Then you ask, and the LLM writes a Tool Call. The host machine runs it and writes the Tool Result. Then the LLM reads that result and writes the answer.","src":"course-images/fundamentals/context-tool-call.webp","alt":"The context of a single tool call","width":1600,"height":893},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Follow the four arrows, because it matters who writes each message:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"You"}]},{"type":"text","text":" write the Human Message."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The LLM"}]},{"type":"text","text":" writes the Tool Call. It is asking, not doing."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The host machine"}]},{"type":"text","text":" writes the Tool Result. Your laptop or your server is the machine that actually has Python installed and a filesystem to read."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The LLM"}]},{"type":"text","text":" reads the whole context, which now includes that result. Then it writes the answer."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Both new messages stay in the context. That is why an agent's context grows so much faster than the context of a chat. One turn can add several messages instead of two."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"why-tools-exist","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Why Tools Exist"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"A model is trained on fixed data. So, on its own, it cannot know anything new, and it cannot change anything. Tools remove both limits."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"It cannot search the web."}]},{"type":"text","text":" So you write a function that takes a query, calls a search engine and returns the results. Now it can."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"It cannot read your files."}]},{"type":"text","text":" So you write a function that takes a filename and returns the contents. Now it can see your code."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Without tools, a model is a chatbot. With tools, it is an agent."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"tool-schema","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Tool Schema"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Here is the part that surprises people."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Before the model can choose a tool, somebody has to tell it three things: that the tool exists, what it does, and what arguments it takes. That description is the "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"tool schema"}]},{"type":"text","text":". It is written in JSON."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"You almost never write that JSON yourself. You write a normal function, and your framework generates the schema from it:"}]},{"type":"code","language":"python","lines":[[{"text":"@tool","role":"function"}],[{"text":"def","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" get_weather","role":"function"},{"text":"(city: str) -> str:"}],[{"text":"    \"\"\"Get the current temperature for a city.\"\"\"","role":"string"}],[{"text":"    return","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" requests."},{"text":"get(","role":"function"},{"text":"f","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":"\"https://api.example.com/weather?city=","role":"string"},{"text":"{","role":"number"},{"text":"city","role":"function"},{"text":"}","role":"number"},{"text":"\"","role":"string"},{"text":")","role":"function"},{"text":".text"}]]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"From that one decorator, the framework reads:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"the "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"name"}]},{"type":"text","text":" from the function name, "},{"type":"code","text":"get_weather"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"the "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"description"}]},{"type":"text","text":" from the docstring"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"the "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"parameters"}]},{"type":"text","text":" from the type hints, so "},{"type":"code","text":"city: str"},{"type":"text","text":" becomes a required string"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Then the framework sends that schema with your request. The model receives it at the start, next to the system prompt. You register the function. The code that connects it to the model is not yours to write."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"This means that the name and the description are not documentation. They are the interface."}]},{"type":"text","text":" They are the only information the model has when it decides which tool to use. Take a function called "},{"type":"code","text":"get_data"},{"type":"text","text":" with the docstring \"gets data\". The model will pick it at the wrong moments and skip it at the right ones. No amount of prompting fixes that. Give the tool a name that says what it does. Write the docstring for the model, because the model is the one that has to choose."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"tool-call-in-action","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Tool Call in Action"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"That is enough description. Here is a real exchange with a weather tool."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"what-the-model-receives","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"What the Model Receives"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The instructions and the tool schema arrive together. In the API, they are separate fields. But from the model's point of view, they are one block at the top of the context:"}]},{"type":"code","language":"text","lines":[[{"text":"SYSTEM"}],[{"text":"You are a helpful assistant with access to tools."}],[{"text":"Answer the user's question. If you need information you do not have, call a"}],[{"text":"tool instead of guessing. Never invent a weather reading."}],[],[{"text":"TOOLS"}],[{"text":"get_weather"}],[{"text":"  Get the current temperature for a city."}],[{"text":"  city (string, required) - The city name, for example \"Istanbul\""}]]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"That readable block is the schema that the framework generated. In the request that goes to the API, it looks like this:"}]},{"type":"code","language":"json","lines":[[{"text":"{"}],[{"text":"  \"type\"","role":"string"},{"text":": "},{"text":"\"function\"","role":"string"},{"text":","}],[{"text":"  \"function\"","role":"string"},{"text":": {"}],[{"text":"    \"name\"","role":"string"},{"text":": "},{"text":"\"get_weather\"","role":"string"},{"text":","}],[{"text":"    \"description\"","role":"string"},{"text":": "},{"text":"\"Get the current temperature for a city.\"","role":"string"},{"text":","}],[{"text":"    \"parameters\"","role":"string"},{"text":": {"}],[{"text":"      \"type\"","role":"string"},{"text":": "},{"text":"\"object\"","role":"string"},{"text":","}],[{"text":"      \"properties\"","role":"string"},{"text":": {"}],[{"text":"        \"city\"","role":"string"},{"text":": {"}],[{"text":"          \"type\"","role":"string"},{"text":": "},{"text":"\"string\"","role":"string"},{"text":","}],[{"text":"          \"description\"","role":"string"},{"text":": "},{"text":"\"The city name, for example ","role":"string"},{"text":"\\\"","role":"number"},{"text":"Istanbul","role":"string"},{"text":"\\\"","role":"number"},{"text":"\"","role":"string"}],[{"text":"        }"}],[{"text":"      },"}],[{"text":"      \"required\"","role":"string"},{"text":": ["},{"text":"\"city\"","role":"string"},{"text":"]"}],[{"text":"    }"}],[{"text":"  }"}],[{"text":"}"}]]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Notice that both the description and the parameter description came from things that you wrote in Python."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"what-you-ask","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"What You Ask"}]},{"type":"code","language":"text","lines":[[{"text":"USER"}],[{"text":"What's the weather in Istanbul right now?"}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"what-the-model-generates","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"What the Model Generates"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"It does not generate prose. It generates this:"}]},{"type":"code","language":"json","lines":[[{"text":"{"}],[{"text":"  \"role\"","role":"string"},{"text":": "},{"text":"\"assistant\"","role":"string"},{"text":","}],[{"text":"  \"content\"","role":"string"},{"text":": "},{"text":"null","role":"number"},{"text":","}],[{"text":"  \"tool_calls\"","role":"string"},{"text":": ["}],[{"text":"    {"}],[{"text":"      \"id\"","role":"string"},{"text":": "},{"text":"\"call_9k2m4Xq7\"","role":"string"},{"text":","}],[{"text":"      \"type\"","role":"string"},{"text":": "},{"text":"\"function\"","role":"string"},{"text":","}],[{"text":"      \"function\"","role":"string"},{"text":": {"}],[{"text":"        \"name\"","role":"string"},{"text":": "},{"text":"\"get_weather\"","role":"string"},{"text":","}],[{"text":"        \"arguments\"","role":"string"},{"text":": "},{"text":"\"{","role":"string"},{"text":"\\\"","role":"number"},{"text":"city","role":"string"},{"text":"\\\"","role":"number"},{"text":":","role":"string"},{"text":"\\\"","role":"number"},{"text":"Istanbul","role":"string"},{"text":"\\\"","role":"number"},{"text":"}\"","role":"string"}],[{"text":"      }"}],[{"text":"    }"}],[{"text":"  ]"}],[{"text":"}"}]]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Three things are worth noticing here. First, "},{"type":"code","text":"content"},{"type":"text","text":" is empty, because the model has not answered yet. It has asked. Second, there is an "},{"type":"code","text":"id"},{"type":"text","text":". This "},{"type":"code","text":"id"},{"type":"text","text":" is how the result gets matched back to this specific call. Third, "},{"type":"code","text":"arguments"},{"type":"text","text":" is a "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"string"}]},{"type":"text","text":" that contains JSON. It is not a JSON object. Almost everyone gets this wrong once."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"what-the-host-machine-does","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"What the Host Machine Does"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"When this message arrives, the framework takes over. It parses the arguments and finds the Python function registered under "},{"type":"code","text":"get_weather"},{"type":"text","text":". Then it calls that function:"}]},{"type":"code","language":"python","lines":[[{"text":"get_weather(city","role":"function"},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":"\"Istanbul\"","role":"string"},{"text":")","role":"function"},{"text":"   # -> '{\"city\":\"Istanbul\",\"temp_c\":34,\"conditions\":\"clear\"}'","role":"comment"}]]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The return value goes back into the conversation as a new message. The framework tags this message with the "},{"type":"code","text":"id"},{"type":"text","text":" from the call:"}]},{"type":"code","language":"json","lines":[[{"text":"{"}],[{"text":"  \"role\"","role":"string"},{"text":": "},{"text":"\"tool\"","role":"string"},{"text":","}],[{"text":"  \"tool_call_id\"","role":"string"},{"text":": "},{"text":"\"call_9k2m4Xq7\"","role":"string"},{"text":","}],[{"text":"  \"content\"","role":"string"},{"text":": "},{"text":"\"{","role":"string"},{"text":"\\\"","role":"number"},{"text":"city","role":"string"},{"text":"\\\"","role":"number"},{"text":":","role":"string"},{"text":"\\\"","role":"number"},{"text":"Istanbul","role":"string"},{"text":"\\\"","role":"number"},{"text":",","role":"string"},{"text":"\\\"","role":"number"},{"text":"temp_c","role":"string"},{"text":"\\\"","role":"number"},{"text":":34,","role":"string"},{"text":"\\\"","role":"number"},{"text":"conditions","role":"string"},{"text":"\\\"","role":"number"},{"text":":","role":"string"},{"text":"\\\"","role":"number"},{"text":"clear","role":"string"},{"text":"\\\"","role":"number"},{"text":"}\"","role":"string"}],[{"text":"}"}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"what-the-model-answers","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"What the Model Answers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Now the whole context goes back to the model:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"the system prompt"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"your question"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"its own tool call"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"the result"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The model reads all of it and writes:"}]},{"type":"code","language":"text","lines":[[{"text":"ASSISTANT"}],[{"text":"It's currently 34°C and clear in Istanbul."}]]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"That is the entire mechanism. Every agent that you will ever build runs these same steps in a loop, again and again."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The exact field names differ a little between providers. But the shape does not change:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"a schema that you did not write"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"a call that the model writes"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"an execution that you perform"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"a result that you give back"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"example-tools","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Example Tools"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Anything that you can write as a function can be a tool. Here are a few real ones:"}]},{"type":"code","language":"python","lines":[[{"text":"@tool","role":"function"}],[{"text":"def","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" run_command","role":"function"},{"text":"(command: str) -> str:"}],[{"text":"    \"\"\"Run a shell command and return its output.\"\"\"","role":"string"}],[{"text":"    return","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" subprocess."},{"text":"run(command, shell","role":"function"},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":"True","role":"number"},{"text":", capture_output","role":"function"},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":"True","role":"number"},{"text":", text","role":"function"},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":"True","role":"number"},{"text":")","role":"function"},{"text":".stdout"}],[],[{"text":"@tool","role":"function"}],[{"text":"def","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" get_current_time","role":"function"},{"text":"() -> str:"}],[{"text":"    \"\"\"Get the current date and time.\"\"\"","role":"string"}],[{"text":"    return","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" datetime.datetime."},{"text":"now()","role":"function"},{"text":"."},{"text":"strftime(","role":"function"},{"text":"'%Y-%m-","role":"string"},{"text":"%d","role":"number"},{"text":" %H:%M:%S'","role":"string"},{"text":")","role":"function"}],[],[{"text":"@tool","role":"function"}],[{"text":"def","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" query_db","role":"function"},{"text":"(sql: str) -> list:"}],[{"text":"    \"\"\"Run a read-only SQL query against the application database.\"\"\"","role":"string"}],[{"text":"    with","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" sqlite3."},{"text":"connect(","role":"function"},{"text":"'database.db'","role":"string"},{"text":")","role":"function"},{"text":" as","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" conn:"}],[{"text":"        return","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" conn."},{"text":"execute(sql)","role":"function"},{"text":"."},{"text":"fetchall()","role":"function"}],[],[{"text":"@tool","role":"function"}],[{"text":"def","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" search_docs","role":"function"},{"text":"(query: str) -> list:"}],[{"text":"    \"\"\"Find the most relevant documentation passages for a question.\"\"\"","role":"string"}],[{"text":"    return","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" vector_db."},{"text":"search(encode(query), top_k","role":"function"},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":"5","role":"number"},{"text":")","role":"function"}],[],[{"text":"@tool","role":"function"}],[{"text":"def","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" send_email","role":"function"},{"text":"(to: str, subject: str, body: str) -> str:"}],[{"text":"    \"\"\"Send an email from the user's account.\"\"\"","role":"string"}],[{"text":"    return","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" gmail."},{"text":"users()","role":"function"},{"text":"."},{"text":"messages()","role":"function"},{"text":"."},{"text":"send(userId","role":"function"},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":"'me'","role":"string"},{"text":", body","role":"function"},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":"compose(to, subject, body))","role":"function"},{"text":"."},{"text":"execute()","role":"function"}]]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Most useful tools are a wrapper around an API."}]},{"type":"text","text":" In that last one, Gmail's own API does nine tenths of the work, and the tool adds only the last tenth. The tool part is a function signature, a docstring, and a call. The same shape can give an agent access to:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"your Jira (create an issue, search, comment)"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"your calendar"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"your payment provider"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"your internal service"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Anything with an API can become something that the model is able to reach. That is usually the fastest way to make an agent useful at work, because the API you need almost always exists already."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Two things follow from this. First, the tool is exactly as powerful as the credentials that you give it. So a tool that can send mail from your account can send anything to anyone. Second, you rarely have to write these tools yourself any more. "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"intermediate/coding-agents","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Code Agents"}]},{"type":"text","text":" covers MCP, which is a standard way to package tools so that one tool works in any agent. With MCP, somebody has already wrapped the API that you were about to wrap."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The docs tool is also worth a second look. It is the RAG pipeline from "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"fundamentals/rag","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"RAG & Embeddings"}]},{"type":"text","text":", turned into a tool. Now the model decides "},{"type":"emphasis","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"when"}]},{"type":"text","text":" to retrieve. You no longer retrieve on every question. That small change is most of what separates a RAG app from an agent."}]},{"type":"diagram","number":"4.3","title":"Example Tools","svg":{"light":"api/v1/diagrams/33ce59957619cde884d9d4f8d19ffa21.svg","dark":"api/v1/diagrams/831cb975e277a2c6645c4a19365edb78.svg"}},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"key-terms","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Key Terms"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Tool"}]},{"type":"text","text":": a function that you write, which the host runs when the model asks."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Context"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the stack of messages that the model reads, and each tool call adds two messages to it."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Tool call"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the message in which the model asks the host to run a tool."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Host"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the machine that runs your functions, such as your laptop or your server."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Tool result"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the message that the host writes with the function's return value."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Tool schema"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the JSON description of a tool: its name, what it does, and its arguments."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"@tool decorator"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the decorator that makes the framework build the schema from the function's name, docstring and type hints."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Tool call ID"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the "},{"type":"code","text":"id"},{"type":"text","text":" that links a tool result to its tool call."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Arguments"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the inputs of a tool call, which the model writes as a JSON string rather than as a JSON object."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"API wrapper"}]},{"type":"text","text":": a tool that adds a small layer on top of an API that already exists."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Credentials"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the access that you give a tool, since a tool is exactly as powerful as its credentials."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"MCP"}]},{"type":"text","text":": a standard way to package tools, so that one tool works in any agent."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"summary","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Summary"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"A tool is a function that you write. The model cannot run it. So the model writes a Tool Call, and your machine runs the function and gives back a Tool Result. Both messages go into the context. That is why agent contexts grow fast."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"You do not write the schema. Your framework generates it from the function, its docstring and its type hints. This makes the name and the docstring the real interface. They are all the model has when it decides which tool to use."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Next comes memory: what happens to all these messages as the context fills up."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"references","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"References"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"-articles","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"📄 Articles"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://machinelearningmastery.com/mastering-llm-tool-calling-the-complete-framework-for-connecting-models-to-the-real-world/"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Mastering LLM tool calling"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the full framework, with more of the wire format than we needed here"}]}]}]}],"draft":null,"quiz":[{"question":"A model decides to call the tool `read_file`. Who runs the function?","options":["The model runs the function inside its own weights.","The framework sends the function to the model, and the model runs it.","The host machine runs the function and writes the Tool Result.","The user runs the function and types the result into the chat."],"answer":2,"explain":"The model has no computer, so it only writes a Tool Call that asks for the function. The host machine, your laptop or your server, runs it and writes the Tool Result."},{"question":"Why does an agent's context grow faster than a chat's context?","options":["Each Tool Call and each Tool Result stay in the context.","Each tool call adds a new system prompt to the context.","The model must write a longer answer after each tool call.","The user must ask the question again after each tool call."],"answer":0,"explain":"A tool call adds two new messages: the Tool Call and the Tool Result. Both stay in the context, so one turn can add several messages instead of two."},{"question":"You write a function with the `@tool` decorator. Where does the framework get the tool's description?","options":["From the function name, `get_weather`.","From the type hints, for example `city: str`.","From a JSON file that you write by hand.","From the docstring of the function."],"answer":3,"explain":"The framework generates the tool schema for you, and it reads the description from the docstring. The name comes from the function name, and the parameters come from the type hints."},{"question":"Your tool is called `get_data` and its docstring says \"gets data\". The model picks it at the wrong moments. What fixes this?","options":["Add more instructions about the tool to the system prompt.","Rename the tool and rewrite its docstring for the model.","Make the code inside the function faster and more correct.","Add longer comments inside the body of the function."],"answer":1,"explain":"The name and the description are the only things the model uses to choose a tool. More prompting does not fix a vague name and docstring."},{"question":"In the Tool Call message, the model writes an `id`, for example `call_9k2m4Xq7`. What is it for?","options":["It tells the host machine which Python file to open.","It counts how many tools the model called in this chat.","It matches the Tool Result back to this specific call.","It identifies the user who asked the question."],"answer":2,"explain":"The host machine tags the Tool Result with the same `id`, in the field `tool_call_id`. This links the result to the call that asked for it."}]}